26
00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:29.390
is that it's really easy for an adult to say,

27
00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:31.930
this book is important, you should follow it.

28
00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:34.790
Well, they're still learning this book

29
00:01:34.790 --> 00:01:37.030
as many of us are as well.

30
00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:39.230
And it's hard for them to really grasp like,

31
00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:41.550
okay, well, what parts do I follow and how do I follow

32
00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:42.750
and what's literal and what's not.

33
00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:44.990
There's just so many other things here

34
00:01:44.990 --> 00:01:47.390
that they often look at us adults as like,

35
00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:49.550
yeah, but you've got it figured out and you can do it.

36
00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:51.230
And I don't need that right now.

37
00:01:51.230 --> 00:01:52.870
I'm young and I can do what I want.

38
00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:54.350
And when I'm your age, maybe I'll do that.

39
00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:58.070
And I get that it's important and I'll get there.

40
00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:02.350
It's more powerful if one of their peers stands up

41
00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:05.870
and declares based on the evidence,

42
00:02:05.870 --> 00:02:07.610
based on what we've studied,

43
00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:09.830
based on the conviction of the Holy Spirit,

44
00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:12.790
I believe this book to be authoritative

45
00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:15.950
and I'm going to respond in that way

46
00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:17.950
and make sure my life is headed down the path

47
00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:19.650
that God wants it to go.

48
00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:21.590
That's a powerful, powerful statement

49
00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:22.990
and a powerful storyteller

50
00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:25.910
if they can sort of recall like how that happened.

51
00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:27.510
And you might have other students

52
00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:29.070
and maybe that is happening to them

53
00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:30.270
and they just either don't realize it

54
00:02:30.270 --> 00:02:32.350
or aren't willing to speak up about it,

55
00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:34.270
that might conjure up some other feelings

56
00:02:34.270 --> 00:02:35.310
from some other students

57
00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:38.230
and give you a little bit of a gauge where you're at.

58
00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:39.670
If you don't have a student,

59
00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:42.870
then I would suggest that that's in that space

60
00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:44.870
that maybe you have a graduate,

61
00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:49.430
someone that has gone through your program

62
00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:52.190
and is now in college

63
00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:54.710
and now that things are kind of rolling along

64
00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:57.590
and they've been in college for a little bit of time,

65
00:02:57.590 --> 00:02:59.350
maybe they're starting to experience some things,

66
00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:01.230
maybe they're starting to see the importance

67
00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:02.230
of what's in here,

68
00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:04.270
especially some of the contradictions

69
00:03:04.270 --> 00:03:06.510
they may be hearing on the college campus,

70
00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:09.950
have them come in and talk about the Bible's authority

71
00:03:11.350 --> 00:03:13.710
and fill that storyteller role.

72
00:03:13.710 --> 00:03:15.170
Okay, so we say it all the time

73
00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:16.630
and we hear it all the time, right?

74
00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:23.060
The Bible is the word of God

75
00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:25.680
and what we're saying is that the Bible is inspired.

76
00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:27.360
The Bible is infallible.

77
00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:29.120
The Bible is the word of God.

78
00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:31.080
It's everything in it is absolutely true.

79
00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:33.160
Therefore, it must be authoritative, right?

80
00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:36.000
But what students will often struggle with

81
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:39.600
and what you'll hear in terms of objections is this,

82
00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:45.680
that the Bible was written by men.

83
00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:50.240
Wait a minute, how can the Bible be authoritative?

84
00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:52.040
And yeah, okay, sure, it can be historical

85
00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:53.680
and okay, yeah, I can fulfill prophecy

86
00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:55.480
and okay, it can be completely accurate

87
00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:57.520
and in some kind of strange way.

88
00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:00.880
But how can it be so authoritative

89
00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:02.160
that we call it the word of God?

90
00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:04.720
That we call it the very commands of God

91
00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:07.640
handed down from him to us

92
00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:11.280
so that we can live the way he has designed us to live

93
00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:13.840
if it was written by men.

94
00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:18.840
How do we know that what Matthew recorded

95
00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:21.240
when he recorded the words of Jesus

96
00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:23.200
that he actually recorded the words of Jesus?

97
00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:25.760
I mean, I probably wouldn't get it right.

98
00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:28.960
I was listening to our sermon just on Sunday

99
00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:31.840
and I tried to write down exactly what the pastor said

100
00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:33.000
because I really liked what he said

101
00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:33.880
in one particular sentence

102
00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:36.920
and I couldn't remember exactly what he said in that moment.

103
00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:40.040
How could we expect Matthew to write down everything

104
00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:42.880
that Jesus said perfectly?

105
00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:45.560
These are the kinds of objections

106
00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:47.520
that you will get from your students.

107
00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:50.840
And it's not a terribly difficult objection to deal with

108
00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:53.280
but just to kind of paint the picture for you

109
00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:55.760
the way I've done it with students in the past

110
00:04:55.760 --> 00:04:57.920
is that think of it this way.

111
00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:00.640
And I'm not making an exact equation

112
00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:03.080
but it helps paint a better picture.

113
00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:08.080
Is you have Jesus and you have the Bible.

114
00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:10.080
Now clearly we don't treat the Bible

115
00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:11.720
the same way we treat Jesus.

116
00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:16.720
However, we very easily say that Jesus is 100% man

117
00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:22.300
and he's also 100% God.

118
00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:25.140
Now wait a minute, if I can comfortably say that

119
00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:28.460
why can't I comfortably say that the Bible was written

120
00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:32.560
by men inspired by God to be completely accurate?

121
00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:37.240
See if God can make this sort of thing happen

122
00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:40.300
God can make this sort of thing happen.

123
00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:41.580
It's pretty simple.

124
00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:43.580
The second objection that you'll have to hear students

125
00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:47.100
make is that the Bible may be accurate and historical

126
00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:48.060
in all of those things

127
00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:54.180
but the Bible is simply a book about God

128
00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:58.780
that it's man's recordings of who God is

129
00:05:58.780 --> 00:06:00.740
or who they thought him to be.

130
00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:03.780
Now that's not accurate either

131
00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:06.340
because if the Bible is authoritative

132
00:06:06.340 --> 00:06:09.540
then sure what we think about God

133
00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:12.820
but it was completely accurate

134
00:06:12.820 --> 00:06:14.740
about who God actually is

135
00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:19.340
because otherwise we don't know then who God really is

136
00:06:19.340 --> 00:06:22.700
but we do know who God really is because he came in Jesus

137
00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:25.400
so we actually do have that picture.

138
00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:27.660
So this is so your students start to see

139
00:06:27.660 --> 00:06:29.820
that although it seems very easy

140
00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:31.820
to say other things about the Bible

141
00:06:31.820 --> 00:06:34.820
which really just allows us to excuse ourselves

142
00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:37.180
on various types of sin

143
00:06:37.180 --> 00:06:41.940
that what the Bible teaches is 100% authoritative.

144
00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:43.980
So in your small groups

145
00:06:43.980 --> 00:06:45.740
as you're thinking through all these things

146
00:06:45.740 --> 00:06:49.180
think through the implications of what all of this means.

147
00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:52.100
These last four weeks what all of this is coming

148
00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:55.140
what conclusions are your students now coming to?

149
00:06:55.140 --> 00:06:58.580
And I want to remember three specific things

150
00:06:58.580 --> 00:06:59.820
that your students can understand

151
00:06:59.820 --> 00:07:01.140
to know truth through all of this

152
00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:03.620
is that one they should have a greater appreciation

153
00:07:03.620 --> 00:07:04.980
for this, right?

154
00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:06.300
So if nothing else

155
00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:08.900
they should have a greater appreciation for the Bible

156
00:07:08.900 --> 00:07:11.620
what it says, what it teaches, how it came together

157
00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:12.820
all of those pieces

158
00:07:12.940 --> 00:07:16.420
and hopefully we'll compel them to read a little bit more.

159
00:07:16.420 --> 00:07:19.460
Secondly, that they'll be more inclined to read this.

160
00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:24.100
How many Christians have read the Bible cover to cover

161
00:07:24.100 --> 00:07:26.060
multiple times?

162
00:07:26.060 --> 00:07:28.700
It's the greatest book,

163
00:07:28.700 --> 00:07:30.820
greatest, biggest selling best selling book

164
00:07:30.820 --> 00:07:32.100
in the history of the world

165
00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:35.100
probably the least read book in the history of the world.

166
00:07:36.620 --> 00:07:39.620
And I think they're more likely to follow.

167
00:07:39.620 --> 00:07:42.700
The more they appreciate the more they'll read

168
00:07:42.700 --> 00:07:44.380
the better they'll follow.

169
00:07:45.620 --> 00:07:49.180
That is the aim of this week.

170
00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:53.420
Now, this has to sort of cross into

171
00:07:53.420 --> 00:07:55.620
we're gonna start talking in the next few weeks here

172
00:07:55.620 --> 00:07:57.660
about what this means in conversation.

173
00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:00.180
When your students are in college

174
00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:01.980
when your students are talking with friends

175
00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:03.180
and it might be sitting reading this

176
00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:05.540
and someone makes a comment.

177
00:08:05.540 --> 00:08:07.380
How do they then defend

178
00:08:07.380 --> 00:08:10.620
and talk intelligently about this biblical text?

179
00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:13.940
And I think over the last four weeks

180
00:08:15.380 --> 00:08:17.860
we've been able to provide the tools

181
00:08:17.860 --> 00:08:19.420
